#
# muir-ah as the associated machine of a System 100 band (DESIGN.md §8).
# A file of flags: a flag a line, its value the rest of the line, and a
# line beginning with # a comment. Name it with -c, or copy it to
# .muir-ahrc where muir-ah is run.
#
# The numbers are the band's own, and a host answering at any others is one
# it never calls. Its host table, sys/site/hosts.text in the release, has
# the band as MIT-LISPM-1 at 3050 and its file and time host as MIT-OZ at
# 3060 (muir's src/chaos/mod.rs). This host is MIT-OZ.

--address 3060

# As the band's own table has this host, sys/site/hosts.text: its names,
# the official first, and its system type. The table's line for MIT-OZ
# ends "SERVER,UNIX,VAX,[OZ]", and the band reads UNIX as the system type
# and VAX as the machine type (GENERATE-HOST-TABLE-2,
# sys/network/chaos/chsaux.lisp:1565-1573). HOSTAB answers this host's
# names with UNIX, and never with a machine type.
--name    MIT-OZ,OZ,system=UNIX

# Where the socket is bound. Without this line, 127.0.0.1:42042: this host
# and nothing else, which is enough for muir runs beside it. To be reached
# from other hosts, an address on the segment, or 0.0.0.0 for every
# interface (DESIGN.md §5):
#--listen 192.0.2.10

# The files. The base root holds the homes --- LOGIN gives a user /<user>/
# --- and is written to. The release's sources are mounted at /tree,
# read-only, because the band asks for them as /tree/sys/... (muir's
# --chaos-file-root help); muir's own vendor/run/file-root links tree to the
# same directory. Each must be a directory when muir-ah starts, and the
# base one it can write (DESIGN.md §6).
--root    /srv/lispm
--root    tree=/path/to/muir/vendor/system-100-0/sys,ro

# The host table HOSTAB answers from, for a name a band's own table lacks.
# The band's machine, as its table has it:
--host    3050,MIT-LISPM-1,CADR-1,CADR1,LM1,system=LISPM
# A second machine takes an address of its own, and a line here so that
# every band can find it by name; its own name is its band's business, in
# SYS: SITE; (DESIGN.md §9). For one:
#--host   3051,MIT-LISPM-2,LM2,system=LISPM

# Endpoints are learned from the packets each host sends. A --peer line
# fixes one that must not move, cbridge's for one:
#--peer   3040@192.0.2.5

# A muir run for this band, on this host (DESIGN.md §9):
#
#   muir --disk-pack /path/to/muir/vendor/run/disk-sys-100-0.img \
#        --chaos-address 3050 --chaos-udp 42043 \
#        --chaos-udp-peer 3060@127.0.0.1:42042
#
# --chaos-address 3050 without its ,<server> half leaves muir's own server
# at its default, 177002, out of the band's way, so the band's calls to
# 3060 come here. A second machine is 3051 on 42044, and so on. Until muir
# has a default peer, each run also names every other machine's address at
# this host's endpoint --- the first --chaos-udp-peer 3051@127.0.0.1:42042,
# the second --chaos-udp-peer 3050@127.0.0.1:42042 --- so that their
# packets to each other come through here (DESIGN.md §5, §9).
